          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. In △ABC, ∠B = 90°, AB = 5, BC = 12 and AC = 13. Find sin A, cos A and tan A. Then find sin C, cos C and tan C.\n\n2. In a right triangle the hypotenuse is 25 and the side opposite to angle θ is 7. Find the adjacent side, and then sin θ, cos θ and tan θ.\n\n3. The two legs of a right triangle are 9 and 12. Find the hypotenuse. For the angle opposite the side of length 9, find sin, cos and tan, and write each in simplest form.\n\n4. Two right triangles have sides 6, 8, 10 and 9, 12, 15. For the smallest angle in each triangle, find tan. What do you notice, and why does it happen?\n\n5. A ramp A rises 3 m over a horizontal run of 15 m. A ramp B rises 2 m over a horizontal run of 8 m. Which ramp is steeper? Show your working using the ratio rise ÷ run.\n\n6. A student claims that in some right triangle, for an acute angle θ, sin θ = 5/4. Is this possible? Explain using the idea of the hypotenuse.\n\n7. In a right triangle, both legs are 2 units long. Find the hypotenuse, and then sin θ, cos θ and tan θ for one of the acute angles. Leave any square root as it is.\n\n8. State True or False:\n   (a) For an acute angle, tan θ can be greater than 1.\n   (b) sin θ = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse.\n   (c) If you enlarge a right triangle keeping its angles the same, the value of tan θ changes.\n   (d) For an acute angle in a right triangle, cos θ is always less than 1.\n   (e) The value of sin θ = 3/5 means the opposite side is 3 cm and the hypotenuse is 5 cm only.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. ∠B = 90°, to hypotenuse AC = 13 (check: 25 + 144 = 169 ✅).\n   Angle A: opposite = BC = 12, adjacent = AB = 5.\n   sin A = **12/13**, cos A = **5/13**, tan A = **12/5**.\n   Angle C: opposite = AB = 5, adjacent = BC = 12.\n   sin C = **5/13**, cos C = **12/13**, tan C = **5/12**.\n\n2. Adjacent² = 25² − 7² = 625 − 49 = 576, to adjacent = **24**.\n   sin θ = **7/25**, cos θ = **24/25**, tan θ = **7/24**.\n   Jaanch: 7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625 = 25² ✅\n\n3. Hypotenuse² = 9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225, to hypotenuse = **15**.\n   Angle jiski opposite 9 hai: adjacent = 12.\n   sin = 9/15 = **3/5**, cos = 12/15 = **4/5**, tan = 9/12 = **3/4**.\n\n4. Sabse chhota angle sabse chhoti side (6 ya 9) ke saamne hota hai.\n   Pehle triangle mein tan = 6/8 = **3/4**. Doosre mein tan = 9/12 = **3/4**.\n   Dono barabar hain. Aisa isliye hota hai kyunki dono triangles ka shape same hai (ek doosre ka chhota-bada photo hain), isliye angle same hai, aur ratio sirf angle par depend karta hai, size par nahi.\n\n5. Ramp A: 3/15 = 1/5 = 0.2. Ramp B: 2/8 = 1/4 = 0.25.\n   0.25 > 0.2, to **ramp B zyada steep** hai.\n\n6. **Nahi, ye possible nahi hai.** sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse. Hypotenuse right triangle ki sabse lambi side hoti hai, to opposite (jo ek leg hai) hypotenuse se chhoti hogi. Isliye ratio 1 se chhota hi aayega. 5/4 to 1 se bada hai (1.25), to ye ho hi nahi sakta.\n\n7. Hypotenuse² = 2² + 2² = 4 + 4 = 8, to hypotenuse = √8 = **2√2**.\n   Kisi bhi ek acute angle ke liye opposite = 2, adjacent = 2.\n   sin θ = 2/(2√2) = **1/√2**, cos θ = 2/(2√2) = **1/√2**, tan θ = 2/2 = **1**.\n   (Ye wahi ratios hain jo Example 6 mein aaye, kyunki ye usi triangle ka double hai. Same angle, same ratio!)\n\n8. (a) **True** ✅. Jaise tan = 12/5 ya 4/3.\n   (b) **False** ❌. sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse. Adjacent ÷ hypotenuse to cos θ hai.\n   (c) **False** ❌. Ratio sirf angle par depend karta hai, size par nahi.\n   (d) **True** ✅. Cos = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, aur adjacent leg hypotenuse se chhoti hoti hai.\n   (e) **False** ❌. sin θ = 3/5 ka matlab bas ye hai ki opposite aur hypotenuse ka ratio 3:5 hai. Ye 3 cm aur 5 cm ho sakte hain, ya 6 cm aur 10 cm, ya 30 m aur 50 m. Ratio ka koi unit nahi hota.\n\nSin, cos aur tan ab tumhare paas hain, aur tumhe pata hai ki ye triangle ke size se nahi, sirf angle se tay hote hain. Jab bhi atko, SOH-CAH-TOA bolo aur pehle apna angle pehchano.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ye teen ratios akele nahi aate. Agar har ratio ko ulta (flip) kar dein, to kya milega? Topic 3 mein yahi dekhenge.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. In △ABC, ∠B = 90°, AB = 5, BC = 12 and AC = 13. Find sin A, cos A and tan A. Then find sin C, cos C and tan C.\n\n2. In a right triangle the hypotenuse is 25 and the side opposite to angle θ is 7. Find the adjacent side, and then sin θ, cos θ and tan θ.\n\n3. The two legs of a right triangle are 9 and 12. Find the hypotenuse. For the angle opposite the side of length 9, find sin, cos and tan, and write each in simplest form.\n\n4. Two right triangles have sides 6, 8, 10 and 9, 12, 15. For the smallest angle in each triangle, find tan. What do you notice, and why does it happen?\n\n5. A ramp A rises 3 m over a horizontal run of 15 m. A ramp B rises 2 m over a horizontal run of 8 m. Which ramp is steeper? Show your working using the ratio rise ÷ run.\n\n6. A student claims that in some right triangle, for an acute angle θ, sin θ = 5/4. Is this possible? Explain using the idea of the hypotenuse.\n\n7. In a right triangle, both legs are 2 units long. Find the hypotenuse, and then sin θ, cos θ and tan θ for one of the acute angles. Leave any square root as it is.\n\n8. State True or False:\n   (a) For an acute angle, tan θ can be greater than 1.\n   (b) sin θ = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse.\n   (c) If you enlarge a right triangle keeping its angles the same, the value of tan θ changes.\n   (d) For an acute angle in a right triangle, cos θ is always less than 1.\n   (e) The value of sin θ = 3/5 means the opposite side is 3 cm and the hypotenuse is 5 cm only.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. ∠B = 90°, to hypotenuse AC = 13 (check: 25 + 144 = 169 ✅).\n   Angle A: opposite = BC = 12, adjacent = AB = 5.\n   sin A = **12/13**, cos A = **5/13**, tan A = **12/5**.\n   Angle C: opposite = AB = 5, adjacent = BC = 12.\n   sin C = **5/13**, cos C = **12/13**, tan C = **5/12**.\n\n2. Adjacent² = 25² − 7² = 625 − 49 = 576, to adjacent = **24**.\n   sin θ = **7/25**, cos θ = **24/25**, tan θ = **7/24**.\n   Jaanch: 7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625 = 25² ✅\n\n3. Hypotenuse² = 9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225, to hypotenuse = **15**.\n   Angle jiski opposite 9 hai: adjacent = 12.\n   sin = 9/15 = **3/5**, cos = 12/15 = **4/5**, tan = 9/12 = **3/4**.\n\n4. Sabse chhota angle sabse chhoti side (6 ya 9) ke saamne hota hai.\n   Pehle triangle mein tan = 6/8 = **3/4**. Doosre mein tan = 9/12 = **3/4**.\n   Dono barabar hain. Aisa isliye hota hai kyunki dono triangles ka shape same hai (ek doosre ka chhota-bada photo hain), isliye angle same hai, aur ratio sirf angle par depend karta hai, size par nahi.\n\n5. Ramp A: 3/15 = 1/5 = 0.2. Ramp B: 2/8 = 1/4 = 0.25.\n   0.25 > 0.2, to **ramp B zyada steep** hai.\n\n6. **Nahi, ye possible nahi hai.** sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse. Hypotenuse right triangle ki sabse lambi side hoti hai, to opposite (jo ek leg hai) hypotenuse se chhoti hogi. Isliye ratio 1 se chhota hi aayega. 5/4 to 1 se bada hai (1.25), to ye ho hi nahi sakta.\n\n7. Hypotenuse² = 2² + 2² = 4 + 4 = 8, to hypotenuse = √8 = **2√2**.\n   Kisi bhi ek acute angle ke liye opposite = 2, adjacent = 2.\n   sin θ = 2/(2√2) = **1/√2**, cos θ = 2/(2√2) = **1/√2**, tan θ = 2/2 = **1**.\n   (Ye wahi ratios hain jo Example 6 mein aaye, kyunki ye usi triangle ka double hai. Same angle, same ratio!)\n\n8. (a) **True** ✅. Jaise tan = 12/5 ya 4/3.\n   (b) **False** ❌. sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse. Adjacent ÷ hypotenuse to cos θ hai.\n   (c) **False** ❌. Ratio sirf angle par depend karta hai, size par nahi.\n   (d) **True** ✅. Cos = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, aur adjacent leg hypotenuse se chhoti hoti hai.\n   (e) **False** ❌. sin θ = 3/5 ka matlab bas ye hai ki opposite aur hypotenuse ka ratio 3:5 hai. Ye 3 cm aur 5 cm ho sakte hain, ya 6 cm aur 10 cm, ya 30 m aur 50 m. Ratio ka koi unit nahi hota.\n\nSin, cos aur tan ab tumhare paas hain, aur tumhe pata hai ki ye triangle ke size se nahi, sirf angle se tay hote hain. Jab bhi atko, SOH-CAH-TOA bolo aur pehle apna angle pehchano.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ye teen ratios akele nahi aate. Agar har ratio ko ulta (flip) kar dein, to kya milega? Topic 3 mein yahi dekhenge."
          }
